TITLE='Authentic title "Why The Middle Class Is Being Taxed Into Poverty" makes a strong causal claim. The script follows a composite family through a year of taxes, premiums, child care and repairs, then argues the system shifted the tax mix from corporations to payroll. Late in the video it concedes "The middle class isn\'t being taxed more overall", contradicting the literal title, and most of the squeeze it shows comes from non-tax costs (child care, premiums, repairs). The budget arithmetic also contains a ~$1,000/month error. TYPICAL against the cohort. Association only.'
THUMB='Registered hqdefault 480x360 (maxresdefault returns HTTP 404; HTTP 200 real image with black letterbox bars). White field. Left: the recurring cartoon man holding a "Taxes" sheet beside piles of cash and coins; a curved arrow points to a ragged, poor version of the same man holding out a hand with coins. Heavy black headline "DON\'T LET THIS HAPPEN". At 320x180 the headline, the before/after figures and the "Taxes" label remain readable. The rich-to-poor transformation matches the "taxed into poverty" title.'
SCRIPT='Single-narrator composite-family narrative: kitchen-table hook; the Andersons ($110K, two kids); paycheck deductions; property tax; federal return and child tax credit; refund spent; FICA; annual tax-plus-premium total; daycare; monthly budget; invisible taxes; AC, braces, tires, transmission debts; parent\'s medical bill; then structural argument (corporate vs payroll share of revenue, corporate rates and zero-tax companies, SS wage cap, capital gains, OECD tax-to-GDP, health/child care/tuition comparisons, Germany, wealth concentration, CEO pay, ProPublica, buybacks, retirement and housing outcomes, lobbying and tax expenditures); December raise vs premium; conclusion. Reproduce: FICA, withholding and refund, property-tax share, $39,886/$62,086 totals, SS cap 2.2%, 59% FICA rise. Errors: paycheck deductions inconsistent with net pay (Mike $637/$528/$463; Sarah $748 vs $621); the $48 monthly surplus is really a $952 deficit; the card balance is double the narrative; 2020 ITEP figures wrong; "75% of shareholders top 1%" wrong; the $2,000 child credit stale for 2025; the federal total uses the pre-credit tax; German benefits overstated. The stored ending is complete.'
